  <h2>What we check when ads “don’t work”</h2>
  <p>
    Failed accounts are often expectation problems, not mysterious Google curses. We look at how long
    campaigns ran, what was promised, and whether the website can convert organic traffic at all. Slow
    or confusing sites get fixed first, that’s the silent killer behind “ads don’t work.”
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the account: incomplete assets, one lazy ad vs multiple tested RSAs, wrong keyword breadth
    for the budget, missing negatives, mobile-only weirdness, spam filters, and conversion/bid setup.
    For most general home-service campaigns we favor a broader “max clicks / shotgun” approach over
    Max Conversions chasing the easiest cheap job types, because homeowners search the trade name,
    not the exact repair.
  </p>

  <h2>When we raise or lower spend</h2>
  <p>
    Budgets follow how busy you are and how much volume you want next, not a weekly vanity ritual.
    More budget doesn’t always mean more booked jobs; sometimes it just means Google gets paid more.
    We test, watch quality, and avoid slamming a winning campaign back into learning with oversized
    increases.
  </p>

  <h2>Meta for customer leads?</h2>
  <p>
    Usually no, not as the primary engine. Facebook leads need heavier follow-up and more
    persuasion; Google catches intent. Meta can still help for long-cycle, high-ticket offers
    (generators, etc.), light brand spend once you’re roughly in the $1-2M range, retargeting, and
    especially hiring.
  </p>
